---
title: 🍔 Turtle Bay Style Halloumi Burger
time: 1 hour
tags:
  - vegetarian
  - burger
  - Caribbean
  - halloumi
  - jerk
  - fried
servings: 4
---

-- Halloumi

Drain @halloumi{2 blocks (500%g total)} and pat thoroughly dry with kitchen paper.

Slice each block lengthways into 2 thick slabs, giving 4 pieces roughly the size of your burger buns.

-- Jerk marinade

Whisk together @unsweetened plant milk{100%ml}, @lime juice{1%tbsp}, @hot sauce{1%tbsp}, @soy sauce{1%tbsp}, @jerk seasoning{1 1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/2%tsp}, @garlic powder{1/2%tsp}, @onion powder{1/2%tsp}, @dried thyme{1/4%tsp}, @ground ginger{1/4%tsp} and @cayenne{1/16%tsp}.

Place the halloumi slabs in the marinade and leave for ~{15%minutes} to ~{20%minutes}. Halloumi doesn't need nearly as long as seitan; too long and the salt in the marinade starts drawing out too much moisture.

-- Crispy fried coating

Mix @plain flour{120%g}, @cornflour{60%g}, @baking powder{1%tsp}, @garlic powder{1%tsp}, @onion powder{1%tsp}, @sweet paprika{1%tsp}, @jerk seasoning{1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/4%tsp}, @black pepper{1/2%tsp}, @white pepper{1/2%tsp}, @salt{1/4%tsp} and @cayenne{1/4%tsp} in a wide bowl. Halloumi is already salty, so this uses less added salt than the seitan version.

In another bowl, mix @unsweetened plant milk{120%ml}, @hot sauce{1%tsp} and @jerk marinade{1%tsp}.

Take a marinated halloumi slab and dredge it dry, then wet, then dry.

On the second dry coating, really press the flour mixture into the surface. This creates the craggy pieces of batter that give you a proper fried-chicken exterior.

For an even more aggressive crust, flick 1 to 2 tablespoons of the wet coating mixture into the dry flour and rub it through with your fingers before coating. This creates little clumps of seasoned flour which become crispy nuggets on the outside.

-- Frying

Heat @neutral frying oil{} in a #frying pan{} to 170–175°C.

Fry the halloumi slabs individually for ~{2%minutes} to ~{3%minutes} per side, until deep golden and crisp. Halloumi is already cooked, so you're only crisping the coating and warming the centre through; frying much longer turns it rubbery.

Transfer the fried pieces to a #wire rack{} rather than kitchen paper.

Don't put the fried pieces directly onto a plate; the steam trapped underneath will soften the crust. A wire rack keeps the bottom crisp.

-- Mild jerk mayo

Mix @mayonnaise{120%g}, @jerk seasoning{1 1/2%tsp}, @lime juice{1%tsp}, @maple syrup{1%tsp}, @light soy sauce{1/2%tsp}, @allspice{1/4%tsp}, @garlic powder{1/4%tsp}, @cayenne{1/16%tsp} and @hot sauce{1/2%tsp}.

Refrigerate for at least ~{30%minutes} before serving.

Taste before assembling. You want creaminess, gentle jerk spice, slight sweetness and lime.

If it tastes too spicy, add more @mayonnaise{} and a tiny amount of @maple syrup{}.

-- Lime-smashed avocado

Mash @ripe avocados{2} roughly with @lime juice{15%ml} and @salt{1/4%tsp}. Don't blend it; you want small pieces of avocado remaining so it feels substantial in the burger.

-- Pink onions

Thinly slice @red onion{1}.

Mix @white vinegar{60%ml}, @water{60%ml}, @sugar{1%tbsp} and @salt{1/2%tsp}, then pour over the sliced onion.

Leave for at least ~{30%minutes}, preferably ~{1%hour}.

Drain thoroughly before putting the onions in the burger.

-- Wild rocket

Wash and thoroughly dry @wild rocket{60%g}.

Keep refrigerated until assembly.

-- Burger buns

Use @soft brioche-style buns{4}.

Toast the cut sides in a #frying pan{} with a tiny amount of @butter{}. You want them golden on the inside and soft everywhere else. Don't toast them until crunchy; the bun needs to compress around the fried halloumi.

-- Assembly

Spread a tablespoon of the mild jerk mayo over each bottom bun.

Add a small handful of @wild rocket{}.

Place one piece of the fried halloumi on top.

Add 2 tablespoons of the smashed avocado.

Add 1 to 2 tablespoons of the pink onions.

Finish with another half tablespoon of the mild jerk mayo and place the top bun over the burger.

Give the top bun a gentle press. Don't squash it flat.

Serve immediately.

-- Restaurant finishing touch

For the best texture, assemble the burger immediately after frying the halloumi. The contrast between the hot crispy coating, warm salty halloumi, cool avocado, creamy jerk mayo, peppery rocket and sharp pink onions is central to the burger.

Serve with @skin-on fries{} tossed with @salt{} immediately after frying.

-- Prep-ahead workflow

Ahead of time, make the mild jerk mayo and pink onions; both keep well in the fridge for a few days.

On the day, marinate the halloumi, bread to order, fry, toast the bun, smash the avocado, dress the rocket and assemble.

Unlike the seitan version, the halloumi needs no overnight prep. It only takes ~{20%minutes} to ~{25%minutes} from marinating to plating.

-- Key points

Don't compromise on @cornflour{} in the coating; it gives you that brittle crunch.

Pat the halloumi thoroughly dry before marinating; too much surface moisture stops the crust from crisping.

Don't over-fry the halloumi; it's already cooked, so you're crisping the coating rather than cooking the centre.

Use @wild rocket{} rather than ordinary lettuce for the peppery flavour.

Use pink onions rather than cucumber pickles for the sharp, acidic element.

No BBQ sauce in this version; the mild jerk mayo alone carries the jerk flavour.
